{"step_index":73,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T21:01:35Z","input_tokens":3421,"cache_read_tokens":71710,"output_tokens":766,"thinking":"The error indicates a CORS policy violation, specifically a blocked cross-origin request. The browser's security policy prevents scripts from a local file (`file:///`) accessing resources on a different origin, which is occurring when trying to load the `firebase-config.js` file. The solution is to either host the HTML file and the JavaScript file on a web server with matching origins or use a browser that allows local file access without CORS restrictions, for example by enabling special flags.\n\n\nThe CORS error stems from the browser's security policy blocking cross-origin requests when accessing local files via `file:///`. ES modules loaded from local files are restricted, preventing them from importing other local files. Considering whether the HTML file can be made self-contained or import Firebase directly from a CDN to function regardless of deployment method.\n\nFurther investigation required to determine if importing from `https://` works on `file:///`, or if the browser blocks ES modules or requires specific script types (UMD/compat/REST). Mandatory research confirms this approach is viable.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"chrome \\\"file:///\\\" script type module import https CORS\""}}]}
